More details on the plan to make the type contextual, so that, for example `.json()` only shows up in intellisense if the Open API spec lists "application/json" under the selected status code and the JSON response wasn't specified earlier in the chain. 

1. Type the response object so that intellisense shows the expected response codes. (this issue)
2. Add descriptions to the response codes when they show up in intellisense ("200_OK", "404_NOT_FOUND") while accepting anything that starts with a valid status code (`200`, `"404_NOT_PETS_FOUND"`)
3. Make the type specific enough that `.headers()` restricts the first argument to valid header names.
4. Make the type specific enough that `.headers()` restricts the second argument to the type matching the header name.
5. Make the type specific enough that `.headers()` doesn't show up if there are no headers left to add.
6. Make the type specific enough that `.match()` restricts the first argument to valid header names.
7. Make the type specific enough that `.match()` restricts the second argument to the type matching the header name.
9. Make the type specific enough that `.match()` doesn't show up if there is no body or all of the media types have been accounted for.
10. Shortcuts to `.match()` for `json()`, `html()`, `text()` and maybe a couple of others.
11. Don't return a valid response object if there are missing headers or media types. 


Regarding that last point, we can probably make that work with the type system and produce a useful error message by adding flags that explicitly make the object invalid. 

